mc_netlink: require CAP_NET_ADMIN and bound the message against the skb

The NETLINK_QCA_MC control socket is created with an all-zero
netlink_kernel_cfg - no NL_CFG_F_NONROOT_* flags, no bind hook - and
netlink_sendmsg() applies netlink_allowed() only when a destination group
or peer portid is set. A sendto() addressed to the kernel therefore
carries no permission requirement at all, and __mc_netlink_receive()
adds none of its own: any uid on the box can drive the whole command set,
including the ACL table and the multicast forwarding policy.

It is also handed the message with no framing check. The handler takes
nlmsg_hdr(skb) and immediately stores into msghdr->status, 40 bytes in,
without ever comparing skb->len against the header it is about to write
through. Worse, msghdr->buf_len - a u32 from the same user buffer - is
passed to mc_acltbl_fillbuf(), mc_mdbtbl_fillbuf() and
mc_rtports_fillbuf() as the size of the *output* area, and each turns it
straight into a write count:

	num_entries = buflen / sizeof(*entry);

The output area is the message skb itself. A short GET_ACL or GET_MDB
carrying a large buf_len therefore writes one record per table entry past
the end of a small allocation - and since the handler works on an
skb_clone() that shares the data buffer, the wrecked skb_shared_info is
later walked by skb_release_data() on the free path. The multicast
database behind GET_MDB grows from IGMP/MLD reports, so the record count
is not bounded by anything the caller had to be trusted with.

Take the sender's capability first, reject a message too short to hold
the header it is about to be written through, and clamp buf_len to what
the skb actually carries. if_name gets terminated because dev_get_by_name()
runs strlen() on it.

The same function's ACL update has a matching defect. The IGMP branch
writes the entry the search found; its MLD sibling writes at the count:

	/* IGMP */ memcpy(&mc->igmp_acl.patterns[i], &pattern, sizeof pattern);
	/* MLD  */ memcpy(&mc->mld_acl.patterns[mc->mld_acl.pattern_count], ...);

Both sit under "if (i != pattern_count)", i.e. a match was found at i, so
the MLD form targets the wrong slot in every case and lands one entry past
struct mc_param_pattern patterns[MC_ACL_RULE_MAX_COUNT] once the table is
full. mld_acl is the last ACL table in struct mc_struct, so those 48 bytes
run into the router-port list head and its queue-entry pointer, both of
which the packet path dereferences. 32 of the 48 come straight from the
caller. Restore parity with the IGMP branch.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>

--- a/mc_netlink.c
+++ b/mc_netlink.c
@@ -120,7 +120,7 @@
 			MC_PRINT(KERN_INFO "%s: Del MLD acl rule, count=%d\n", __func__, mc->mld_acl.pattern_count);
 		} else {
 			if (i != mc->mld_acl.pattern_count) {
-				memcpy(&mc->mld_acl.patterns[mc->mld_acl.pattern_count], &pattern, sizeof pattern);
+				memcpy(&mc->mld_acl.patterns[i], &pattern, sizeof pattern);
 				MC_PRINT(KERN_INFO "%s: Update acl rule\n", __func__);
 				goto out;
 			}
@@ -546,6 +546,23 @@
 	u32 pid, seq, msgtype;
 	struct __mcctl_msg_header *msghdr;
 	struct mc_struct *mc = NULL;
+	u32 datalen;
+
+	/*
+	 * The socket is created with no flags and no bind hook, and
+	 * netlink_sendmsg() runs its own permission test only for a group or
+	 * peer destination - a message to the kernel skips it. Nothing else
+	 * below stands between an arbitrary uid and the command set.
+	 */
+	if (!netlink_capable(__skb, CAP_NET_ADMIN))
+		return;
+
+	/*
+	 * Everything below indexes off nlmsg_hdr() without ever consulting
+	 * skb->len, starting with an unconditional store into msghdr->status.
+	 */
+	if (__skb->len < NLMSG_LENGTH(0) + MC_MSG_HDRLEN)
+		return;
 
 #if (LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(3, 10, 0))
 	if ((skb = skb_clone(__skb, GFP_ATOMIC)) == NULL)
@@ -563,6 +580,20 @@
 	msgdata = MC_MSG_DATA(nlh);
 	msgtype = nlh->nlmsg_type;
 
+	/*
+	 * buf_len is the caller's claim about the reply area, which the
+	 * fillbuf helpers divide by the entry size to get a write count. That
+	 * area is this skb, so the skb is the bound.
+	 */
+	datalen = skb->len - (NLMSG_LENGTH(0) + MC_MSG_HDRLEN);
+	if (msghdr->buf_len > datalen)
+		msghdr->buf_len = datalen;
+
+	/*
+	 * dev_get_by_name() runs strlen() on this.
+	 */
+	msghdr->if_name[IFNAMSIZ - 1] = '\0';
+
 	do {
 		/*
 		 * For global option to set, it needn't specified bridge
